1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,300
Three cameras, three streams, one cable. If
the signals share the

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:14,600
same path, why don't they become one
unreadable mess? The answer

3
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:21,900
depends on a rule that makes combining them
reversible.

4
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:27,267
First separate two ideas. The medium is the
physical path; a

5
00:00:27,267 --> 00:00:32,633
channel is a way of using that path. One cable
can

6
00:00:32,633 --> 00:00:38,000
support several channels when the equipment
gives their signals distinguishable places

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:43,367
or turns.

8
00:00:43,367 --> 00:00:50,800
At one end, the multiplexer combines the
inputs. At the other,

9
00:00:50,800 --> 00:00:58,233
the demultiplexer separates them. The
important ingredient sits between those names:

10
00:00:58,233 --> 00:01:05,667
both ends must agree on exactly how the
sharing works.

11
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:10,925
A wave changes over time. Frequency counts its
cycles each second;

12
00:01:10,925 --> 00:01:16,183
bandwidth describes a range of frequencies.
Shift your attention to the

13
00:01:16,183 --> 00:01:21,442
frequency view: it shows where each signal
occupies part of that

14
00:01:21,442 --> 00:01:26,700
range.

15
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:34,000
Place each stream in a different frequency
band. Now all three

16
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:41,300
can travel at once because their assigned
bands differ. Frequency division

17
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:48,600
makes simultaneous sharing possible by
separating signals along the frequency axis.

18
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:53,967
Why leave gaps between the bands? Real filters
have gradual edges,

19
00:01:53,967 --> 00:01:59,333
so neighboring signals could interfere. Guard
bands provide space between them.

20
00:01:59,333 --> 00:02:04,700
The price of cleaner separation is spectrum
that carries no channel

21
00:02:04,700 --> 00:02:10,067
here.

22
00:02:10,067 --> 00:02:15,542
At the receiver, select the green band. A
bandpass filter passes

23
00:02:15,542 --> 00:02:21,017
that range and reduces others. The green
stream doesn't wait for

24
00:02:21,017 --> 00:02:26,492
the rest to finish; the receiver separates it
by frequency while

25
00:02:26,492 --> 00:02:31,967
they coexist.

26
00:02:31,967 --> 00:02:39,267
Three bands need twenty kilohertz each. Two
gaps need five each.

27
00:02:39,267 --> 00:02:46,567
Add the occupied widths: twenty, five, twenty,
five, twenty. Seventy kilohertz

28
00:02:46,567 --> 00:02:53,867
covers this arrangement, before any extra
margins at the outer edges.

29
00:02:53,867 --> 00:03:01,156
Now one camera goes quiet. Its reserved band
stays empty while

30
00:03:01,156 --> 00:03:08,444
the others keep their allocations. The same
clear separation that makes

31
00:03:08,444 --> 00:03:15,733
this scheme simple can leave capacity unused
when traffic becomes uneven.

32
00:03:15,733 --> 00:03:21,100
Instead of separating streams by frequency,
separate them by time. Let

33
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:26,467
each source use the shared path during an
assigned turn. The

34
00:03:26,467 --> 00:03:31,833
receiver identifies the source by when its
data appears in the

35
00:03:31,833 --> 00:03:37,200
schedule.

36
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:42,567
Follow the order: A, B, C, then repeat. The
combined stream

37
00:03:42,567 --> 00:03:47,933
alternates between sources, and the receiver
reconstructs three outputs from those

38
00:03:47,933 --> 00:03:53,300
positions. What looks mixed in transit becomes
separate again by following

39
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:58,667
the schedule.

40
00:03:58,667 --> 00:04:04,250
One complete round of slots forms a TDM frame
in this

41
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:09,833
example. Here, frame means a repeating timing
group. The same word

42
00:04:09,833 --> 00:04:15,417
appears elsewhere in networking with other
meanings, so keep this particular

43
00:04:15,417 --> 00:04:21,000
structure in view.

44
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:26,475
The schedule only works if both ends agree on
its boundaries.

45
00:04:26,475 --> 00:04:31,950
Shift the receiver's timing and it assigns
data to the wrong

46
00:04:31,950 --> 00:04:37,425
source. Synchronization is part of the sharing
mechanism, not an optional

47
00:04:37,425 --> 00:04:42,900
finishing touch.

48
00:04:42,900 --> 00:04:48,475
Each camera produces two megabits per second.
Together they require six

49
00:04:48,475 --> 00:04:54,050
megabits per second of useful shared capacity.
A practical link needs

50
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:59,625
room for synchronization and other overhead
too; useful data isn't the

51
00:04:59,625 --> 00:05:05,200
whole transmitted stream.

52
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:10,675
When B has no data, its assigned turn still
arrives. That

53
00:05:10,675 --> 00:05:16,150
empty slot reveals the weakness of a fixed
schedule: capacity can

54
00:05:16,150 --> 00:05:21,625
go unused even while other sources have work
waiting to be

55
00:05:21,625 --> 00:05:27,100
sent.

56
00:05:27,100 --> 00:05:32,575
Give turns only to sources with data, and
those empty positions

57
00:05:32,575 --> 00:05:38,050
can disappear. That's statistical TDM. The
receiver now needs identifiers or

58
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:43,525
equivalent shared information, since a fixed
repeating order no longer tells

59
00:05:43,525 --> 00:05:49,000
the whole story.

60
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,583
Change the medium to fiber. Several optical
channels can share a

61
00:05:54,583 --> 00:06:00,167
strand, represented here by different colors.
The drawing tracks their identities;

62
00:06:00,167 --> 00:06:05,750
real systems select particular optical
wavelengths rather than relying on our

63
00:06:05,750 --> 00:06:11,333
display colors.

64
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:16,700
Wavelength division carries those optical
channels together using different wavelengths.
They

65
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:22,067
travel simultaneously through the same fiber.
The sharing resembles frequency separation,

66
00:06:22,067 --> 00:06:27,433
now implemented with light and the optical
equipment needed to handle

67
00:06:27,433 --> 00:06:32,800
it.

68
00:06:32,800 --> 00:06:40,089
At the far end, optical components separate
the combined wavelengths into

69
00:06:40,089 --> 00:06:47,378
their outputs. Without that final separation,
combining channels wouldn't help. The

70
00:06:47,378 --> 00:06:54,667
receiver completes the rule that made sharing
one strand useful.

71
00:06:54,667 --> 00:07:00,358
Four channels at ten gigabits per second each
produce forty gigabits

72
00:07:00,358 --> 00:07:06,050
per second of raw combined rate. The
multiplication is simple; interpreting

73
00:07:06,050 --> 00:07:11,742
it matters. Application throughput can be
lower after the system's overhead

74
00:07:11,742 --> 00:07:17,433
is included.

75
00:07:17,433 --> 00:07:24,589
More channels don't remove the limits of the
medium. Light can

76
00:07:24,589 --> 00:07:31,744
weaken, pulses can spread, and noise can
disrupt recovery. Multiplexing organizes

77
00:07:31,744 --> 00:07:38,900
available resources; it doesn't create an
unlimited, perfectly clean connection.

78
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:46,200
Compare the pictures. FDM separates frequency
bands; TDM separates time slots;

79
00:07:46,200 --> 00:07:53,500
WDM separates optical wavelengths. Each gives
the receiver a different clue

80
00:07:53,500 --> 00:08:00,800
for reconstructing the original inputs after
they shared a path.

81
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:06,267
The right arrangement depends on the traffic.
Steady streams may fit

82
00:08:06,267 --> 00:08:11,733
fixed allocations, while bursts benefit from
serving active sources. Equipment and

83
00:08:11,733 --> 00:08:17,200
physical constraints matter too. There isn't
one sharing rule that wins

84
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:22,667
in every setting.

85
00:08:22,667 --> 00:08:28,250
The three cameras never needed three separate
cables. They needed a

86
00:08:28,250 --> 00:08:33,833
separation rule both ends understood. Remember
the bands, turns, and wavelengths:

87
00:08:33,833 --> 00:08:39,417
sharing works when the receiver can still tell
which information belongs

88
00:08:39,417 --> 00:08:45,000
to whom.
